Custom editors: Allow setting different priorities for edit/diff/merge

This is a rephrasing of https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/138525. 

We are working on a custom-editor extension for `.custom.md` files that allows interacting with markdown outside the IDE's normal text editor, including buttons, checkboxes, and paragraphs that morph into textareas when you click them.

The `.custom.md` format is normal markdown format, but our vscode extension overlays it with a webview so you can interact with checkboxes, plus buttons, and accept buttons to modify the underlying file.

One point of frustration with our custom editor is merging/diffing. While we absolutely want this editor to be the default editor for `.custom.md` files, it does not currently support merging/diffing very well.

So, for merging/diffing, we would strongly prefer to use the text editor by default. However, there does not appear to be such an option. Defaults are controlled by `contributes.customEditors.priority` in `package.json`. For example:

```
  "contributes": {
    "customEditors": [
      {
        "viewType": "custom-markdown.editor",
        "displayName": "Custom Markdown Editor",
        "selector": [
          {
            "filenamePattern": "*.custom.md"
          }
        ],
        "priority": "default"
      }
    ]
  }
```

`priority: default` causes edit, diff, and merge to use the custom editor by default. `priority: option` causes them to use the text editor by default. We feel it would be too inconvenient/unexpected to make users select the custom editor each time they want to edit a `.custom.md` file. 

I tried a fair amount of workarounds, like:

```
    "configurationDefaults": {
      "workbench.editorAssociations": { "*.custom.md": "custom-markdown.editor" }
    },
    "customEditors": [
      {
        "viewType": "custom-markdown.editor",
        "displayName": "Custom Markdown Editor",
        "selector": [
          {
            "filenamePattern": "*.custom.md"
          }
        ],
        "priority": "option"
      }
    ],
```

But nothing resolved the issue.  A proposed alternative would be to replace the `priority` option with:

```
"editPriority": "default",
"diffPriority": "option",
"mergePriority": "option"
```

Would it be possible to add this feature? Thanks!

